{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794269551,"nonce":"8ygLC7WY5UcURmRyKIMssbZhL3g=","title":"Frame cost after a click on the Tasks form: incremental_update is half of it; the builder is 2%","message":"Measured in the live probe (nested kwin_wayland --virtual, real input through org_kde_kwin_fake_input, fs-tasks 0.0.3 release build, blinc_app 0.6.0 with PR 71 applied, one machine). Timestamps are taken in the app (microseconds) around the phases of the desktop frame; the button press is stamped in the injector.\n\nClick on a status button in the task form (result ChildrenChanged, repeated: 2 runs, 3 clicks, all within 5 percent):\n- builder (user closure, 'invoke_ui_builder'): 0.17 ms\n- RenderTree::incremental_update: 4.9 to 5.1 ms\n- full apply_stylesheet_base_styles + apply_stylesheet_layout_overrides (ChildrenChanged branch): 1.7 ms\n- compute_layout: 0.4 ms\n- rest of the rebuild branch (flip, motion, css animations): 0.8 ms\n- render phase (p4): 2.7 ms\n- whole frame: 10.5 ms (phase 2 rebuild: 7.6 ms)\nRelease to end of frame: 11 ms. The window is small (the task form, a few hundred nodes at most), and the Calendar month view (LayoutChanged, a different path) shows the same ratio: builder 0.4 ms, incremental_update 2.6 ms, style 0.74 ms, layout 0.47 ms, whole rebuild frame about 6 ms.\n\nSo the cost of an update that reruns the builder is dominated by the diff and in-place prop update, not by building or layout. That agrees with the 17.6 ms for 3003 nodes in the review of PR 71. Candidates, not yet measured one by one: DivHash::compute_element_tree runs on the whole new element tree and again per node in update_render_props_in_place (compute_element + compute_element_tree + compute_topology_tree per node, so hashing is quadratic in depth); element.render_props() is built twice per node (once in the hash, once for the props).\n\nProposal for the maintainer (no PR before agreement): hash each element once per update and reuse it for the comparison, the stored hash and the changed/unchanged decision.","files":null}]}